From f95cfa15256d7280dde160aa568dd9e4b530de79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logx <iamvarun158@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:03:37 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Ship Align on Android (ai.desertant:align)

Align had no Android build because the Android core has no NFC: its host
bridge only offers NFKC, and the lexical bytes must be NFC to match training.
The Kotlin SDK now composes every word with java.text.Normalizer (Form.NFC)
before writing the payload, and Lexical takes the text as already composed on
Android (trim only). NFC and that trim commute, so the bytes are the ones the
Apple and Linux paths compute.

- Catalog: an .android files entry (the LiteRT stages plus the sidecars), and
  the AlignAndroid dynamic product in Package.swift.
- packages/align-kotlin: Align/AlignNative over the shared LoadedModel ABI,
  WordTiming in and out, the same payload schema as Binding.swift and
  align-node, JVM codec tests and an on-device suite.
- LexicalNFCTests: shared NFC vectors (expected values from a third
  implementation) that Foundation, the Android lexical path and
  java.text.Normalizer are all held to, plus an end-to-end check that
  decomposed and composed spellings refine identically.
- manifest/docs: Kotlin SDK live; docs re-rendered.
- Tools/dal.sh: DAL_SWIFT_SDKS_PATH lets a host keep the Swift SDK bundles
  outside the home directory.
